+# BLOCKED: `test_sla` cannot pass as written
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`WordFreqTests.test_sla` in `test_wordfreq.py` requires `top_words()` to process the
+4 MB reference corpus in **under 1 ms**:
+
+```python
+self.assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)
+```
+
+This is not achievable for any implementation that actually examines the input text.
+It is a hard performance floor, not a missing optimisation.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The reference corpus built by `corpus()` is:
+
+- **4,083,137 characters** (~4 MB)
+- **600,000 word tokens**
+- 5,000 distinct vocabulary words
+
+Timings below are `time.perf_counter()` minima over 7 runs on the project machine
+(macOS aarch64, Python 3.12.5):
+
+| Operation | Min time |
+|---|---:|
+| `len(text)` (does not inspect contents) | ~0.000 ms |
+| `text.lower()` **alone** | **1.42 ms** |
+| `text.lower().split()` | 20.7 ms |
+| compiled `[a-z']+` `findall(text.lower())` | 60.8 ms |
+| `Counter(findall(text.lower()))` | 99.1 ms |
+
+The first unavoidable step — case-folding the 4 MB string required by the
+"compared case-insensitively" contract — already costs **1.42 ms**, i.e. ~1.4x the
+entire SLA, before tokenising or counting a single word. The full operation is
+~100 ms, roughly **100x** over budget. Even a hand-written C implementation would
+still have to scan 4 MB and hash 600,000 tokens; the tokenisation and counting
+alone are far above 1 ms.
+
+I deliberately did **not** work around this by caching results keyed on the text,
+returning a lazy object whose work happens after the timer stops, or editing,
+skipping, or loosening `test_sla`. Those would fake the SLA rather than meet it and
+would leave the search team with a false performance guarantee.
+
+## What was fixed anyway
+
+`top_words()` previously used an O(tokens × vocabulary) linear search over a Python
+list (`w in seen`, `seen.index(w)` for all 600,000 tokens). It now uses
+`collections.Counter` for linear-time counting plus a single sort:
+
+```python
+counts = Counter(_WORD_RE.findall(text.lower()))
+return sorted(counts.items(), key=lambda p: (-p[1], p[0]))[:n]
+```
+
+- Runtime on the SLA corpus: **6.35 s → 99 ms minimum (~64x faster)**.
+- Output is unchanged: word runs of letters/apostrophes, case-insensitive,
+  ties alphabetical.
+- `test_basic`, `test_case_and_apostrophes`, and `test_ties_alphabetical` pass.
+- `test_sla`'s *correctness* assertion passes; only `assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)`
+  fails.
+
+## What needs to change to unblock
+
+Pick one, ideally with the search team:
+
+1. Set a realistic SLA for this corpus (e.g. < 150 ms on the reference machine), or
+2. Express the SLA per KB / per token rather than for a 4 MB document, or
+3. Move the heavy work to an indexing/build step and define the SLA for queries
+   against precomputed counts, or
+4. Shrink the reference corpus so that the intended 1 ms budget is meaningful.
+
+Until one of those happens, `test_sla` will fail by design and no honest
+implementation of `top_words` can make it pass.
